    "question": {
      ":question/id": "ed3c0529-9135-578f-87a0-910c1b30b331",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ when $f(x)=(-6-5x)^{4}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=g^{4},\\,g=-6-5x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=4g^{3},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=-5.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} &=4g^{3}\\cdot (-5) \\\\  &=-20(-6-5x)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
